Actually, let me look at the `VerifyColormapIndex` function and the 2-bit and 4-bit cases more carefully.

For the 2-bit case:
```c
shift = 8;
for (x=0; x < image->columns; x++)
{
    shift -= 2;
    index=(IndexPacket) (3-((*p >> shift) & 0x03));
    VerifyColormapIndex(image,index);
    indexes[x]=index;
    if (shift == 0)
    {
        shift = 8;
        p++;
    }
}
```

For 2-bit, `packets = (2 * columns / 8) * rows = (columns/4) * rows`. The loop reads `ceil(columns/4)` bytes per row. If `columns` is not a multiple of 4, extra bytes are read.

For the 4-bit case:
```c
shift = 8;
for (x=0; x < image->columns; x++)
{
    shift -= 4;
    index=(IndexPacket) (15-((*p >> shift) & 0x0f));
    VerifyColormapIndex(image,index);
    indexes[x]=index;
    *q++=image->colormap[index];
    if (shift == 0)
    {
        shift = 8;
        p++;
    }
}
```

For 4-bit, `packets = (4 * columns / 8) * rows = (columns/2) * rows`. The loop reads `ceil(columns/2)` bytes per row. If `columns` is odd, extra bytes are read.

So the vulnerability is that when `columns` is not a multiple of `8/bits_per_pixel`, the pixel reading loop reads uninitialized bytes from the `pixels` buffer. These uninitialized bytes are used to compute `index`, which is then used to index into the colormap and write to the image.

